.TH BYEBYTE 1 "2026-07-25" "byebyte 0.11.0" "byebyte manual"
.SH NAME
byebyte \- verb CLI for the byebyte storage daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B byebyte
.RI [ verb ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B byebyte
talks to
.BR byebyted (8)
over its control socket and prints the answer. With no verb, it runs
.BR status .
Every verb accepts
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B status
Headroom, burn rate, ETA-to-full, and quota per mount.
.TP
.BI why " [path]"
Instant du-tree from the index, age-tagged (big AND stale stands out).
Accepts
.B \-\-limit
.I N
(default 15). On a
.I btrfs
mount, prints a note when snapshots pin space the walk can't see (or when
btrfs accounting is unavailable).
.TP
.B blame " [\-\-since T]"
What grew since
.IR T ,
a join of two index snapshots.
.I T
is
.IR Nh / Nd / Nw
or
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ;
defaults to
.IR 1d .
Gains the same
.I btrfs
note as
.IR why .
.TP
.B scan
Kick off an on-demand index refresh (otherwise nightly).
.B \-\-wait
blocks until it finishes.
.TP
.BI purge " category " "[\-\-yes]"
Delete what a compiled-in category detector positively matches. Dry-run
by default; pass
.B \-\-yes
to execute.
.B \-\-all
is refused \(em one category per act. Categories: hf-hub, pip-cache,
uv-cache, thumbnails, project-artifacts, rotated-logs, journald,
snap-old. Every deletion is appended to
.IR /var/lib/byebyte/ledger.jsonl .
.TP
.BI declare " path " "[\-\-yes]"
Delete exactly this one path \(em declared scope, not a category. Dry-run
by default; pass
.B \-\-yes
to execute. Scanning stays broad, but what the daemon may eat is named by
the human, one path at a time, from what
.BR why / blame / advise
already surfaced. Refused unconditionally, regardless of config: the
owner's
.B $HOME
or any ancestor of it (this alone covers
.IR / " and " /home ),
any mount point, byebyte's own runtime/state directories, symlinks, and a
compiled-in list of FHS system directories
.RI ( /etc ", " /usr ", " /var ", " /bin ", " /lib ", " /proc ", " /sys ", "
.IR /run ", " /root ", " /srv ", " /opt ", " /mnt ", " /media ).
Also refused: anything outside every configured
.I scan_roots
entry, and declaring a configured root wholesale. Re\-verified immediately
before deletion, not just at dry\-run time \(em a path that changed since
it was declared is skipped, not deleted, and ledgered
.IR skipped .
.TP
.BI reserve " mountpoint " "[percent] [\-\-yes]"
ext2/3/4's own privileged\-writer headroom
.RI ( tune2fs \ \-m ).
With no
.IR percent ,
reports the mount's current reserved\-blocks percentage. Applies
immediately to a mounted filesystem \(em no unmount, no reboot; the
change is visible in the very next
.IR statvfs (2)
call, which is exactly how it's verified: read available space before,
apply, read again, and if it didn't move, the act is reported as failed
regardless of
.BR tune2fs 's
own exit code.
.B Never
accepts a percentage below 1, even with
.BR \-\-yes :
.B tune2fs
itself accepts 0% silently, and refusing it here is the only guard
against a full disk locking out even root. Only ext2/ext3/ext4 mounts
are supported; anything else is refused by name.
.TP
.BI journal-cap " size " "[\-\-yes]"
Bound journald's own persistent disk footprint
.RI ( size
like
.IR 500M " or " 1G ).
Dry-run by default; pass
.B \-\-yes
to apply. Writes a
.I journald.conf.d
drop-in
.RI ( SystemMaxUse= )
and reloads
.B systemd\-journald
live (SIGHUP\-style config reload, never a restart \(em logging is never
interrupted). Also runs
.B journalctl \-\-vacuum\-size=
so a shrunk cap is visibly true right away, with one disclosed caveat:
.B \-\-vacuum\-size
only ever touches archived journal files, never the currently\-active one,
so usage can still exceed the new cap by up to one active segment's worth
until it rotates on its own. Undo: delete the drop-in and
.B systemctl reload systemd\-journald.service .
.TP
.BI fstrim-schedule " [on|off] " "[\-\-yes]"
Turn the stock weekly TRIM timer
.RI ( fstrim.timer ,
shipped by
.IR util\-linux )
on or off. No custom configuration \(em only the shipped unit's own
enablement is touched. With no
.IR on / off ,
reports the current state and, if enabled, when it next runs. Applies
immediately
.RI ( systemctl \ enable / disable \ \-\-now ),
never deferred: the very next
.B systemctl is\-enabled
read proves it.
.TP
.BI tmp-size " size " "[\-\-yes]"
Cap
.IR /tmp 's
own tmpfs size via a
.I tmp.mount
drop-in
.RI ( size
like
.IR 2G " or " 512M ).
Preserves every other mount option
.RI ( mode ", " nosuid ", " usrquota ", " \.\.\. )
verbatim, changing only
.IR size= .
.B PENDING,
not done: the write is instant, but this verb never remounts
.I /tmp
itself \(em the new cap takes effect only at the next reboot, or a manual
.B systemctl daemon\-reload && systemctl restart tmp.mount .
A response never claims success; it reports the write and the still\-
unchanged live total side by side. Refused by name, not attempted blind, on
a system where
.I /tmp
isn't managed via
.IR tmp.mount .
.TP
.B ghosts
List deleted-but-open files still holding disk blocks, grouped by the
process holding them and the mount they live on. Report-only \(em never
signals anything.
.TP
.B ballast " [release]"
Show the pre-allocated emergency reserve, or release it to free space
even at 0 bytes free.
.TP
.B kernels
List removable kernel packages (installed, not running, not newest) and
print the exact
.I apt autoremove
line. Never runs
.BR apt (8)
itself.
.TP
.B advise
One-line nudges: mounts nearing full, fast index growers, cold caches,
heavy ghosts, quota headroom running low.
.TP
.B burn " [\-\-seconds N] [\-\-limit N]"
Live per-process write rates, sampled over
.I N
seconds (default 5, clamped 1\(en30). When fanotify is available (root
only; CAP_SYS_ADMIN), each writer also names the directory it's writing
to, background-aggregated as events arrive \(em not resampled per call.
Absent the capability, writers are named by pid only, exactly as before.
.TP
.B sweep " [\-\-dry] [\-\-history [\-\-limit N]]"
The unattended reclaim policy. Double consent: the
.I byebyte\-sweep.timer
unit (disabled by default) is the opt-in to run at all; per-category arming
via
.I sweep_categories
in
.I config.json
(a subset of
.IR purge 's
own compiled-in categories \(em config can never widen it) is the opt-in to
actually
.B act
rather than preview. An unarmed category, or the whole call under
.BR \-\-dry ,
only ever previews and ledgers a
.I dry_run
entry; only armed categories delete anything, and every act is ledgered.
Kernel removal is always report-only here, regardless of config \(em running
.BR apt (8)
unattended is a separate, not-yet-made decision, kept out of this verb on
purpose.
.I project\-artifacts
is the one category whose preview is skipped, not computed, while unarmed:
its detector is a live walk of the whole
.I scan_roots
tree rather than a handful of fixed-path checks, the same cost
.B advise
already excludes itself from. Arm it to see real candidates.
.B \-\-history " [\-\-limit N]"
replays past sweep acts and previews from the ledger (default 15, most
recent first).
.TP
.B notify " [\-\-dry]"
Delivers a desktop notification for whatever is newly worth an
unprompted interruption, driven by
.I byebyte\-notify.timer
(enabled by default, every 5 minutes) and gated by
.I notify_categories
in
.IR config.json .
Unlike
.B sweep
this never deletes anything and never shares its double-consent gate: a
category worth speaking is either a threshold crossing (a mount entering
.BR warn / hot / edquot ,
quota headroom dropping under 20%) or a named, specific discovery (a fast
index grower, a cold cache, heavy ghosts) \(em never a bare number, never a
forecast that hasn't earned its confidence.
.I mount_alert " , " quota_low " , " fast_grower
are armed by default;
.I eta_soon " , " cold_cache " , " ghosts_heavy
are opt-in. Each category speaks once per crossing and stays silent while
the condition holds, never repeating every timer tick.
.B \-\-dry
prints what would be sent without sending it, and without consuming the
notification opportunity it previewed \(em the next real run still sees it.
.TP
.B accounting " [\-\-write] [\-\-notify]"
The stock report, distinct from every other verb here: everything else
measures a RATE (burn, ETA, thresholds); this measures accumulated,
unmoving material a rate can never trip an alarm for. Three tiers, always
rendered separately and in this order \(em CONDEMNED, COLD, UNKNOWN:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B CONDEMNED
\(em currently just Trash
.RI ( ~/.local/share/Trash ),
sized and dated from the freedesktop trash spec's own
.I DeletionDate
field (the RECORDED decision, not an inferred one from file mtime).
.IP \(bu 2
.B COLD
\(em extends
.BR purge 's
own cache-category detectors (hf\-hub, pip\-cache, uv\-cache, thumbnails,
rotated\-logs), gated on last\-ACCESS time past
.I accounting_cold_days
rather than
.BR advise 's
own mtime\-based heuristic.
.IP \(bu 2
.B UNKNOWN
(large & old)
\(em anything else from the index at or above
.I accounting_large_bytes
and past
.IR accounting_cold_days ,
not already covered by the first two tiers. Claims NO confidence \(em a
large, cold directory is not the same fact as safe to remove, and this
tier never blurs the two (on a machine with real project trees, this is
the longest and least useful section, by design).
.RE
.PP
Every cited item (fire\-marshal ruling d602032b) carries a full citation,
not a bare size: its
.B owner
(the nearest ancestor git repo's name, else the first path segment under
.BR $HOME ,
else
.IR unclassified \(em
never a guessed service name with no evidence behind it), its
.B cost
in context (what fraction of its mount it is, and how many days of that
mount's current growth it represents), and a fixed, tier\-appropriate
.B remedy
phrase. Nothing here is ever a specific claim of certainty or a
recommendation past that placement. An item under
.I accounting_cite_floor_bytes
still counts toward its tier's total but isn't individually cited \(em
the report ends its
.B cold
/
.B unknown
sections with a "+N more below the cite floor, X total" line instead,
so the sum stays honest without the noise of a kilobyte\-sized entry
printed beside a real finding.
.PP
Last\-access is sampled (bounded, not a full recursive walk) and a
mount's atime reliability is checked and disclosed:
.I noatime
marks a reading as unreliable, and
.I relatime
(the common Linux default) marks it as coarse \(em both are said outright
rather than silently trusted.
.PP
Report\-only: no
.BR \-\-yes ,
no act path, never wired to
.BR purge / sweep / declare .
.B \-\-write
persists the run to
.IR /var/lib/byebyte/accounting.json
(what
.I byebyte\-accounting.timer
calls, weekly \(em the "fire marshal walks the building on a schedule,"
not a monitor); an interactive run always computes fresh either way.
.B \-\-notify
sends at most ONE summary toast per run, and only when the report's own
headline (the single most valuable finding: an actionable total when one
exists, else the largest named\-but\-unactionable one, else an honest
"nothing to report") differs from the previous
.I accounting.json \(em
crossing\-once against a FILE rather than in\-memory state, so a marshal
who found nothing new stays silent instead of repeating itself every
week.
.TP
.B policy " [report] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-apply] [\-\-json]"
Storage Sense: applies the written policy in
.I /etc/byebyte/policy.json
and leaves a receipt. Unlike
.BR sweep ,
this runs unattended
.B ON BY DEFAULT
via
.IR byebyte\-policy.timer \(em
"Linux desktops ship no janitor" is the pitch, and an opt-in janitor
reproduces the exact "byebyte feels useless" problem
.B notify
already exists to avoid. Safety instead lives in policy.json's own
.I dry_run
field, read fresh on every run (never cached at daemon startup): it
ships
.BR true ,
so the first run on a fresh install only previews and writes a receipt.
.B \-\-dry\-run
can only ever force a preview \(em it can never force a real run past
what the file itself says.
.PP
The policy names one rule per category, each with its own
.B enabled
flag and
.BR params :
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B trash
\(em empties Trash entries whose
.I DeletionDate
(the same freedesktop\-spec field
.B accounting
cites, never mtime) is older than
.IR older_than_days " (default 30)."
.IP \(bu 2
.B cold_cache
\(em drops the same five cache categories
.B accounting
samples, idle past
.IR idle_days " (default 90)."
Unlike accounting (which only DISCLOSES atime reliability), a
.I noatime
mount makes this rule REFUSE to act there at all \(em it cannot tell idle
from unknown, and the receipt says so per item.
.IP \(bu 2
.B journal
\(em caps journald via the same mechanism as
.BR journal\-cap ,
to
.IR cap_bytes " (default 500M)."
.IP \(bu 2
.B apt_cache
\(em runs
.B apt\-get clean
once
.I /var/cache/apt/archives
exceeds
.IR cap_bytes " (default 500M);"
apt has no partial vacuum, so this is a trigger threshold, not a trim
target.
.IP \(bu 2
.B docker_dangling_images
\(em
.B docker image prune \-f
(dangling only, never
.BR \-a ).
.IP \(bu 2
.B docker_dangling_volumes
\(em same, for volumes, but
.B disabled by default
even after
.I dry_run
is flipped: a dangling volume can be the only copy of real data, unlike
a dangling image's pure rebuildable cache. Enable it deliberately.
.IP \(bu 2
.B snap_revisions
\(em reuses
.BR sweep 's
own
.I snap\-old
detector as\-is (whatever
.BR snap (8)
itself already marked disabled). Each disabled revision is sized from
its own squashfs image file, one stat, never a directory walk; a
revision whose image can't be found is reported as its own count with
size UNKNOWN, never blended into the total as a false 0 bytes.
.RE
.PP
A category name outside this list fails the whole file to load, loudly
\(em policy.json is meant to be read as ground truth, so a typo must
never run silently as if it said nothing. There is no
.I unknown_large
category and never will be: accounting's tier 3 has no act() at all,
by construction, not by policy.
.PP
Runs on two cadences: weekly
.RI ( byebyte\-policy.timer ),
and the instant any mount crosses into
.B hot
(the same threshold
.B mount_alert
uses), triggered from inside
.BR byebyted (8)
itself, crossing\-once. Every run \(em either cadence \(em writes a
receipt to
.IR /var/lib/byebyte/policy_runs/ ,
capped at 500 files (oldest pruned first), plus a
.I latest.json
copy.
.B policy report
prints the latest receipt without running anything.
A real run (not
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
and policy.json's own
.I dry_run
is
.BR false )
sends at most one desktop toast, only when something was actually
freed. The receipt's own
.I policy_file_dry_run
field always names policy.json's OWN configured value, distinct from
.I dry_run
(what THIS particular run actually did) only when a caller asked for a
preview while the file itself says real \(em a reader can tell "this
previews because the file is in dry run" from "this previews because
someone asked for a fresh check first," never confusing the two.
.B \-\-apply
acts on EXACTLY a previously\-shown dry pass's own matched lists, read as
JSON from stdin \(em never a fresh detect: build a
.B \-\-dry\-run
.B \-\-json
doc first, then feed its per\-category
.I matched
arrays (plus a bare true/false per singleton category: journal,
apt_cache, docker_dangling_images, docker_dangling_volumes) back in
unchanged. Refused outright, before touching anything, while
policy.json's own
.I dry_run
is
.BR true .
.TP
.B update " [\-\-check] [\-\-json]"
Delegates straight to
.BR byebyte\-update (8).
.B \-\-check
reports whether a newer release exists (notify tier, the daily timer's own
invocation); a bare
.B update
is the manual\-install consent tier. Never runs unattended \(em no
.B \-\-auto
tier is wired up from this CLI.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ byebyte status
byebyte v0.5.0   (status 3s old)
  /           824G free of   1.8T   burn quiet       full \(em
  /tmp         24G free of    30G   burn quiet       full \(em   [quota: 24.4G of 24.4G left]

$ byebyte why /var/log
612M in /var/log   (scanned 2h ago)
    480M  3d          /var/log/journal
    120M  12d         /var/log/apt

$ byebyte purge pip-cache
     1.2G  /home/asuramaya/.cache/pip
1.2G would be freed (dry-run \(em add \-\-yes to execute)

$ byebyte ghosts
    24.1G  pid 2046892  chrome           12334 fd(s)  on /tmp

$ byebyte burn \-\-seconds 3
    13.3M/s   pid 638912  rsyslogd          (writing on /)
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/byebyte/status.json
The read seam \(em mounts, burn rate, index state. Mode 0640.
.TP
.I /run/byebyte/control.sock
The command socket. Mode 0660, peercred-gated.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BYEBYTE_RUNTIME_DIR
Overrides
.I /run/byebyte
(status.json and control.sock live here). Used for testing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if the daemon is unreachable, or if the daemon answers with an
error (bad arguments, refused category, etc.) \(em the message explains why.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR byebyted (8),
.BR byebyte-healthcheck (1),
.BR byebyte-update (1)
.SH AUTHOR
asuramaya and byebyte contributors.
